WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.640
Back in June at AI DevCon in London,
we spent a lot of time on writing code

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:09.240
with agents, but somebody still has to run
the thing at three in the morning,

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:12.880
and that turned out to be where
some of the most interesting stories were.

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:16.680
Let's first hear from a panel
we put together at short notice

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:21.480
with Stéphane Jourdan, Simon Rohrer and Pini
Reznik on what changes

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:25.240
when everybody in the company is shipping
through an agent around the clock.

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:31.120
So now you have agents by everyone,
from everyone doing things in production.

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:34.120
How do you know the impact of this
24 hours a day?

00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:39.320
It's constant and you really need
proactive agents and all the context.

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:40.800
What changed yesterday?

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:44.720
What was the relationship between
this service and this other service?

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:48.720
How do you know that this service
was actually connecting to the right thing?

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:49.800
But the variable was not.

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:51.880
The name was the IP, that sort of stuff.

00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:54.320
So it's not only about like root
cause analysis.

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:59.640
It's really about like the, the context,
all the knowledge you can have from prod

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:03.680
your multiple accounts
that all the stuff and agents today

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:07.520
you mentioned
like DevOps is a bit back in,

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:11.640
in, in the space because the DevOps folks
had a lot of tools, a lot of

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:14.600
data to work with the code, etc.

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:19.480
but for prod
we really need to keep the lights up.

00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:22.760
So yeah, that data
and that context is your point

00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:27.000
these days, including agents
to fix this 24 hours a day.

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:30.240
Because even from a security perspective,

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:35.240
all the things that are being patched
as well, constantly, you need to ensure

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:38.480
that the blast radius of things
are still working after this.

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:43.040
So everything, everything is
so is going so quickly these days.

00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:46.400
We need — we need that data proactively.

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:49.560
Simon,
I can see you're violently agreeing.

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:52.440
So I work in a regulated industry.

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:55.560
We can't quite move as fast,
but we're still moving pretty fast. I,

00:01:57.040 --> 00:02:00.840
like I said, I've been spearheading this
and sort of coaching developers through.

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:03.840
And I think one of the things for me
that surprised the developers or,

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:05.120
you know, we do "you build it, you run it".

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:07.480
So it is really developer operators.

00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:09.040
The thing that has surprised

00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:13.480
our developers the most is how useful
the agents are for diagnosing production.

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:16.920
Yes, genuinely throw them at Elastic logs

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:21.480
plus ServiceNow incident reports,
plus the code and the combination.

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:24.600
Like you said, is
this knowledge that the agents can have

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:27.760
they will solve an issue in 30 seconds

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:30.960
that could have taken developers hours

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:37.040
or maybe even days to fully understand
this stuff is so powerful, you know,

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:37.920
and I think the people

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:41.800
who are just using it
in the development context really should,

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:43.960
if they have that data available to them,
like they say,

00:02:43.960 --> 00:02:48.200
if they've got the good observability
stack in place, should start to expand

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:51.280
there.
They use it more broadly into this.

00:02:51.320 --> 00:02:55.280
And I think, you know,
we are living through a seismic shift.

00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:57.920
We we don't know where
we're going to end up.

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:01.320
We don't know what the earthquake
will do to us We also heard

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:06.040
from May Walter, co-founder of Hud,
and hers is my favorite kind of talk.

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:08.800
One about something that went wrong.

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:12.200
They automated the investigation,
shipped pull requests

00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:16.320
for every high-impact fix, and discovered
nobody wanted them.

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:21.200
So they rebuilt the output to convince
the human instead of the agent.

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:24.560
So we said, okay,
maybe we can just like add open

00:03:24.560 --> 00:03:27.720
pull requests
for all of these high-impact, low-

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:31.600
risk changes that don't require migrations
or anything super scary.

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:33.880
And we can just open a pull request.

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:38.840
No, no, no, no, no,

00:03:38.840 --> 00:03:42.320
we don't want any pull requests
that no one's going to go over

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:46.760
and we don't want to go over
those 80 PRs even though they exist.

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:50.080
And I think that's like if I
if you can take one thing out of

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:53.240
this is like after you get it working
and you want to automate it,

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:57.880
think about the scale and think about
the humans that are still in the loop.

00:03:57.880 --> 00:04:00.640
We're not at the point
where no one just cares about it.

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:04.160
And if you open a pull request, it's
kind of like opening your Datadog

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:08.520
and Sentry with those like 700 issues
that all they say is like, well,

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:13.200
I'm not going to be able to fix this,
so I'm not even going to try.

00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:16.800
So we of course, we started
with automated pull requests.

00:04:16.840 --> 00:04:19.200
We realized no one cares about that.

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:23.560
And if it's not prioritized, then they're
not going to do something about it.

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:25.760
And again, I am not complaining.

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:27.920
I'm not judging.

00:04:27.920 --> 00:04:29.000
We're builders, right?

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:32.000
We have prioritization
and it exists for a reason.

00:04:32.680 --> 00:04:36.640
No one has time to just go over a bunch
of pull requests built by the agent

00:04:36.640 --> 00:04:40.960
that are statistically huge,
and try to understand what happens.

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:42.080
We don't want to own that.

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:44.200
We don't want to go into it.

00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:49.200
So we actually need to convince the human
that it's worth the attention.

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:51.040
Instead of convincing the agent

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:54.120
that it's worth the tokens,
it will always think that they are.

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:57.560
So we map the hot paths, the endpoints

00:04:57.560 --> 00:05:00.600
that are invoked often
the business impact of that.

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:04.560
So is this impacting payments
or authentication

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:07.920
or the set of things that you already know
that you care about for your business?

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:10.600
And also the risk?

00:05:10.600 --> 00:05:14.240
We are not looking
for the best optimizations.

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:20.000
We are looking for the highest-impact,
lowest-risk changes that can be done

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:23.880
so that we can go to the developer
or the PM and say, hey, listen,

00:05:23.920 --> 00:05:28.160
it's like that small
and it's going to do like 30% improvement.

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:32.640
Amit Kushwaha is a principal solutions
architect at NVIDIA,

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:35.640
and he has a nice,
counterintuitive finding.

00:05:35.880 --> 00:05:40.400
While your agent is off making a tool
call, the GPU is sitting idle.

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:42.360
Account for that properly

00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:46.600
and you can serve about twice
as many users as your benchmark predicted.

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:50.720
The other
thing that you need to think about.

00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:53.720
So I have two colorful blocks here.

00:05:54.280 --> 00:05:57.120
Different rows are basically user,

00:05:57.120 --> 00:06:00.080
user one, user two, user three, user four.

00:06:00.080 --> 00:06:05.120
And what this slide is trying to convey
is if you ignore the tools

00:06:05.160 --> 00:06:08.160
tool part of it,
you're not really measuring the right

00:06:09.040 --> 00:06:10.880
workload again.

00:06:10.880 --> 00:06:14.680
So on the left side
I'm assuming there is no tool call.

00:06:14.680 --> 00:06:18.640
So there is none of that purple block
that I showed where the

00:06:19.520 --> 00:06:22.000
kind of things are running on the CPU.

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:25.840
In that case,
what you ended up saying is user one, user

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:27.000
two, user three, user four

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:30.240
are fully packing my elements
or fully packing my GPUs.

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:35.200
So this whole block is basically when
your large language model is being called.

00:06:36.280 --> 00:06:36.760
But in

00:06:36.760 --> 00:06:39.880
reality, that's not the case
when you are running close.

00:06:39.960 --> 00:06:42.040
This is not how your GPUs look like.

00:06:42.040 --> 00:06:45.760
You're not continuously kind
of keeping your GPUs busy.

00:06:45.960 --> 00:06:52.320
What is happening is you run your large
language models and then actually

00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:56.320
you are running tool calls
which are which are running on your CPU.

00:06:56.320 --> 00:06:59.360
So actually your GPU is not doing any work
during that time.

00:07:00.160 --> 00:07:03.440
And depending on how big that variation

00:07:03.440 --> 00:07:08.120
is, you can actually support more users
because you are effectively

00:07:08.120 --> 00:07:12.240
not packing GPUs as much as you can.

00:07:12.840 --> 00:07:17.360
So just ignoring the concept
that there is no tool actually ends up

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:21.240
predicting the wrong concurrency
or wrong workload

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:24.560
that that
that you can support on your hardware.

00:07:25.240 --> 00:07:27.720
And there are some metrics there, like
if you assume

00:07:27.720 --> 00:07:30.720
one call takes one second,

00:07:30.720 --> 00:07:34.520
if you have a tool call that takes
one second, two and half of your areas,

00:07:35.200 --> 00:07:38.760
you can actually support twice
the number of users than you thought,

00:07:39.040 --> 00:07:42.560
because a bunch of that work
load is being done on CPU too.

00:07:42.560 --> 00:07:47.160
And to close, Justin Cormack,
formerly CTO at Docker.

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:48.760
His single most useful

00:07:48.760 --> 00:07:52.920
debugging investment was getting the
AI to build him a tracing framework

00:07:53.480 --> 00:07:57.400
Hand an agent a rare bug
with no reproduction, and it guesses,

00:07:57.520 --> 00:08:01.680
Hand it a trace from an overnight run
and it finds the thing

00:08:02.240 --> 00:08:06.760
I discovered that like even 
just getting the AI to build a

00:08:07.080 --> 00:08:11.880
hand-built, hand-maintained tracing
framework was incredibly useful.

00:08:11.920 --> 00:08:14.920
You don't need to tie it
into a production system or something,

00:08:14.920 --> 00:08:18.680
but anything that can give it traces
that it can look at to debug

00:08:18.720 --> 00:08:20.680
is amazingly useful.

00:08:20.680 --> 00:08:25.960
It in this case
it had a bunch of overheads,

00:08:25.960 --> 00:08:30.800
so when I used it for performance testing
it was a little bit misleading.

00:08:30.800 --> 00:08:34.720
But it told it,
you know, basically gave where the the big

00:08:34.800 --> 00:08:37.800
the big performance gaps were.

00:08:38.120 --> 00:08:43.320
And it was incredibly useful
for debugging because I could give it,

00:08:43.440 --> 00:08:46.320
you know, I could run the test,

00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:49.640
I could have my test suites running,
looking for race conditions or errors,

00:08:49.720 --> 00:08:55.560
give it a trace and say this happened
overnight in my overnight run.

00:08:55.560 --> 00:08:57.080
We need to fix this.

00:08:57.080 --> 00:08:58.080
And it would

00:08:58.080 --> 00:08:59.600
it would let it actually lock down on

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:02.000
what the real problem
was rather than trying to guess.

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:06.240
Because if you if you give an AI a bug

00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:12.000
but you don't know how to repro it,
and it's a very it's a rare condition.

00:09:12.560 --> 00:09:14.160
It can waste a lot of time either.

00:09:14.160 --> 00:09:18.040
I mean, it can either fail
to reproduce itself or it can guess what

00:09:18.040 --> 00:09:20.240
the solution might be
and get it wrong or something.

00:09:20.240 --> 00:09:24.840
And if you can give it a trace
and some trace tooling

00:09:24.840 --> 00:09:28.680
and just get it to sit there
and try and reproduce it

00:09:28.680 --> 00:09:32.920
and itself and see if it's the same thing,
then it usually can.

00:09:32.960 --> 00:09:34.360
And that works really well.

00:09:34.360 --> 00:09:38.360
So you don't need to necessarily 
hook it up to a production environment.

00:09:38.360 --> 00:09:40.360
You can really do this

00:09:40.360 --> 00:09:44.200
just by building, by getting the
AI to build some tracing tools for you.

00:09:45.520 --> 00:09:46.960
Thanks for watching.

